            <div xml:id="d843">

                <pb facs="0891" n="793" xml:id="pg793"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1812: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-09T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 9,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 9—10:18
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>364. Your 122, May 22, 3 p.m. Consideration [for] American business urgently
                    requires at least working settlement of American municipal rights and
                    obligations Harbin. In view of recognized Chinese altered status <hi
                        rendition="#italic">vis à vis</hi> new railway agreement and position of
                    Russians, I venture to suggest that American consul Harbin be empowered in
                    consultation with Chinese to draft between the two Governments agreement for the
                    approval of Department. Harbin Taoyin recently in Peking intimated his
                    willingness for early settlement.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
